{"candidates":[{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The preference for better-corroborated theories is defensible on Popper's own terms as a methodological rule to maximize informational content and risk exposure, not as an inductive inference of future probability.","counterconditions":["If Popper explicitly states that choosing a corroborated theory implies a belief in its future reliability or probability.","If the distinction between 'acting on' a theory and 'believing' it to be true collapses in Popper's practical advice.","If the concept of verisimilitude is shown to rely on inductive assumptions about the structure of the world."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[{"block":"182a58bdd39650d7"},{"block":"08c37be0a0e09d91"},{"block":"0db48ad23b546413"}],"mechanism":"Popper distinguishes between the logical status of a theory (which remains unproven) and the methodological decision of which theory to test or act upon. Choosing the best-corroborated theory is choosing the one that has survived the severest tests and thus possesses the highest empirical content. This choice is a 'rational guess' aimed at truth (verisimilitude) without claiming that past success guarantees future performance, thereby avoiding the smuggled induction criticized by opponents.","optional_refs":["8023406c46a4fd92bff6c432fdd61fdef4170b236aa54944d798c726ed22164b","61cf23a94f6a53679d5dad7e239a61feb40620590a6ee4afac012a9752037209"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.85}],"simulation_proposals":[]}